In Turkey, Law No. 2911 on Assemblies and Marches (the "Assembly Law") historically required prior notice. The ECtHR has found violations in similar cases (e.g., Yazar v. Turkey, 2008; Şahin v. Turkey, 2010). The Court has examined whether the law is compatible with Article 11. The law's requirement of prior notice is permissible if it is not disproportionate. However, the law also allows police to prohibit assemblies if they anticipate public order issues. The Court looks at whether the authorities gave adequate warnings and whether the decision to disperse was proportionate.

Here, the police said the demonstration was unlawful because advance notice had not been submitted (F013). The law indeed requires notice (F031). So the police action is based on law. The question is whether the law is "prescribed by law". The law exists, but is it sufficiently accessible? The law is published, but the requirement of signature of all board members may be burdensome. However, the ECtHR hasn't declared such requirement invalid per se. The more crucial point is whether the law provides sufficient safeguards against arbitrary application. The law states that force can be used only after warning (F035). The police did warn via loudspeaker. So perhaps the law meets the criteria.

